\lessonlabel{1}
\section{What Accounting Is}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item state the main purpose of accounting;
  \item identify the major users of financial information;
  \item write and explain the accounting equation.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Purpose of accounting}

\term{Accounting} is the systematic process of recording, classifying, summarising, and interpreting financial transactions of an entity. Its purpose is to provide reliable information about the entity’s economic performance and financial position so that decisions can be made.

\begin{example}
A small bakery records daily sales, purchases of flour, and wages paid. At the end of the month the accountant prepares a profit‑and‑loss statement showing whether the bakery earned a profit. This information tells the owner whether the business is viable and where costs can be reduced.
\end{example}

\subsection{Users of financial information}

Financial information is used by several groups:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Owners and investors} – assess profitability and decide on further investment.
  \item \term{Creditors and lenders} – evaluate the ability to repay loans.
  \item \term{Management} – plan operations, control costs, and set prices.
  \item \term{Government and regulators} – ensure compliance with tax and reporting laws.
  \item \term{Employees} – gauge job security and negotiate wages.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A bank reviews a company’s balance sheet before approving a loan. The balance sheet shows the amount of assets available to secure the loan and the level of existing debt.
\end{example}

\subsection{The accounting equation}

The fundamental relationship in accounting is the \term{accounting equation}:

\[
\text{Assets} = \text{Liabilities} + \text{Equity}
\]

It states that everything a business owns (assets) is financed either by borrowing (liabilities) or by the owners’ investment (equity). The equation must hold after every transaction.

\begin{example}
A retailer purchases inventory worth \$5,000 on credit. Assets (inventory) increase by \$5,000 and Liabilities (accounts payable) increase by the same amount, keeping the equation balanced.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Verify that after each transaction the total value of assets equals the sum of liabilities and equity. If the equality does not hold, an error has been made in recording the transaction.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Explain in one sentence why providing reliable profit information is a core purpose of accounting.
  \item List three different users of financial statements and the specific information each needs.
  \item A company receives \$10,000 cash from a bank loan. Show how this transaction affects the accounting equation.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Accounting & Recording, classifying, summarising, and interpreting financial transactions. \\
Purpose of accounting & To provide reliable information for decision‑making about performance and position. \\
Users of financial information & Individuals or groups who rely on accounting reports (owners, creditors, management, government, employees). \\
Assets & Resources owned or controlled by an entity that have economic value. \\
Liabilities & Obligations owed to outsiders that require future outflows of resources. \\
Equity & The residual interest of owners in the assets after deducting liabilities. \\
Accounting equation & The identity Assets = Liabilities + Equity that must hold at all times. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{2}
\section{Recording Transactions}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item identify common source documents for business transactions;
  \item explain the principle of double‑entry bookkeeping;
  \item distinguish between debits and credits and apply them to simple transactions.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Source documents}

\term{Source documents} are the original papers or electronic records that prove a transaction has occurred. Typical examples are:
\begin{itemize}
  \item sales invoice,
  \item purchase receipt,
  \item bank statement,
  \item cash register tape,
  \item payroll slip.
\end{itemize}
Each document contains the date, the parties involved, the amounts, and a brief description. The accountant records the information from the source document in the accounting system.

\begin{example}
A shop sells a laptop for \$800. The sales invoice shows:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Date: 12 March 2026
  \item Customer: Jane Doe
  \item Item: Laptop
  \item Amount: \$800
\end{itemize}
The accountant uses this invoice to enter the sale in the ledger.
\end{example}

\subsection{Double‑entry bookkeeping}

\term{Double‑entry bookkeeping} requires that every transaction affect at least two accounts, keeping the accounting equation balanced:
\[
\text{Assets} = \text{Liabilities} + \text{Equity}
\]
One account is recorded as a \term{debit} and another as a \term{credit}. The total of debits must equal the total of credits for each transaction.

\begin{example}
The shop receives \$800 cash for the laptop sale. The entry is:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Debit \textit{Cash} \$800 (asset increases)
  \item Credit \textit{Sales Revenue} \$800 (equity increases)
\end{itemize}
Debits (\$800) equal credits (\$800), so the equation stays balanced.
\end{example}

\subsection{Debits and credits}

\term{Debit} (Dr) means an entry on the left side of an account. It increases assets and expenses, and decreases liabilities, equity, and revenue.

\term{Credit} (Cr) means an entry on the right side of an account. It increases liabilities, equity, and revenue, and decreases assets and expenses.

\begin{example}
The shop purchases office supplies on credit for \$150.
\begin{itemize}
  \item Debit \textit{Office Supplies} \$150 (asset increase)
  \item Credit \textit{Accounts Payable} \$150 (liability increase)
\end{itemize}
Again, total debits equal total credits.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Record the following transaction using double‑entry bookkeeping: The shop pays \$200 cash for a utility bill.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item List three different source documents you might encounter in a retail business and state what information each provides.
  \item For each of the following, write the debit and credit accounts and amounts:
    \begin{enumerate}
      \item The shop sells goods on credit for \$500.
      \item The shop receives a \$1,000 loan from a bank.
    \end{enumerate}
  \item Explain why the total of debits must always equal the total of credits in every transaction.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{|p{3.5cm}|p{10.5cm}|}
\hline
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\hline
Source document & Original record (paper or electronic) that proves a transaction occurred. \\
\hline
Double‑entry bookkeeping & Accounting method where each transaction is entered as equal debits and credits in at least two accounts. \\
\hline
Debit (Dr) & Left‑hand entry; increases assets/expenses, decreases liabilities/equity/revenue. \\
\hline
Credit (Cr) & Right‑hand entry; increases liabilities/equity/revenue, decreases assets/expenses. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{3}
\section{The General Ledger}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define a \term{ledger account} and describe its purpose;
  \item explain how to post a transaction to the appropriate accounts;
  \item calculate the balance of an account and determine whether it is a debit or credit balance.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Ledger accounts}

A \term{ledger account} records all changes to a single type of asset, liability, equity, revenue or expense. Each account has two sides: a \term{debit side} on the left and a \term{credit side} on the right. For example, the Cash account records every increase (debit) and decrease (credit) of cash.

\begin{example}
The Cash account starts with a balance of \$0. A cash sale of \$500 is entered as a debit of \$500 on the left side. Later the business pays a utility bill of \$120 in cash; this is entered as a credit of \$120 on the right side. The account now shows a total debit of \$500, total credit of \$120, and a net balance of \$380.
\end{example}

\subsection{Posting transactions}

\term{Posting} means transferring the amounts from a source document (e.g., a journal entry) to the appropriate ledger accounts. Each transaction has at least one debit and one credit, and the total debits must equal total credits.

The steps are:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Identify the accounts affected.
  \item Determine the debit amount and the credit amount.
  \item Write the debit amount in the left column of the debit account and the credit amount in the right column of the credit account.
  \item Record the date and a brief description in each account.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}
A company purchases equipment for \$2,000 on credit. The journal entry is:
\[
\text{Equipment} \quad \text{Debit } \$2{,}000 \\
\text{Accounts Payable} \quad \text{Credit } \$2{,}000
\]
Posting places \$2,000 on the debit side of the Equipment account and \$2,000 on the credit side of the Accounts Payable account.
\end{example}

\subsection{Balancing an account}

To find the balance of an account, add all debits and all credits separately, then subtract the smaller total from the larger total. The side with the larger total shows the balance type.

\[
\text{Balance} = \begin{cases}
\text{Total Debits} - \text{Total Credits}, & \text{if Debits > Credits (debit balance)}\\[4pt]
\text{Total Credits} - \text{Total Debits}, & \text{if Credits > Debits (credit balance)}
\end{cases}
\]

\begin{example}
The Supplies account shows total debits of \$800 and total credits of \$300. Because debits are larger, the balance is a debit of \$500 (\$800‑\$300). This amount appears on the left side of the account as the closing balance.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Given a journal entry, list the accounts, indicate which side each amount belongs to, post the amounts, and compute the resulting balance of each affected account.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Create a ledger account for \textit{Bank} and record: (a) a cash receipt of \$1,200, (b) a bank payment of \$450. Show the posted entries and the final balance.
  \item A service is performed on credit for \$3,500. Identify the accounts, post the transaction, and state the balance type of each account after posting.
  \item The Rent Expense account has total debits of \$2,200 and total credits of \$200. Compute the balance and state whether it is a debit or credit balance.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Ledger account & Record that tracks all debits and credits for one specific item (asset, liability, etc.). \\
Debit & Entry on the left side of an account; increases assets and expenses, decreases liabilities, equity, and revenue. \\
Credit & Entry on the right side of an account; increases liabilities, equity, and revenue, decreases assets and expenses. \\
Posting & The process of transferring amounts from journal entries to the appropriate ledger accounts. \\
Balance & The net amount left in an account after total debits and credits are compared; shown as either debit or credit. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{4}
\section{The Trial Balance}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item prepare a trial balance from ledger balances;
  \item identify common errors that cause a trial balance not to balance;
  \item apply simple techniques to locate and correct those errors.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Preparing a trial balance}

A \term{trial balance} is a list of all ledger account balances, with debit balances in one column and credit balances in another. The total of the debit column must equal the total of the credit column.

Steps to prepare a trial balance:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item List every account name from the general ledger.
  \item Write the ending balance of each account in the appropriate column (debit or credit).
  \item Add the debit column and the credit column.
  \item Compare the two totals. If they are equal, the trial balance is balanced.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}
The ledger shows the following balances at month‑end:

\begin{tabular}{lrr}
Account & Debit & Credit\\
Cash & 12,000 & \\
Accounts Receivable & 5,000 & \\
Supplies & 1,200 & \\
Accounts Payable & & 4,500\\
Capital & & 13,700\\
\end{tabular}

Place the balances in the trial‑balance format:

\begin{tabular}{lrr}
Account & Debit & Credit\\
Cash & 12,000 & \\
Accounts Receivable & 5,000 & \\
Supplies & 1,200 & \\
Accounts Payable & & 4,500\\
Capital & & 13,700\\
\midrule
Total & 18,200 & 18,200\\
\end{tabular}

Both totals are equal, so the trial balance is correct.
\end{example}

\subsection{Finding and correcting errors}

If the trial balance does not balance, one or more errors are present. Common sources are:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Transposition error}: digits are reversed (e.g., 540 written as 450).
  \item \term{Slide error}: a digit is misplaced by a place value (e.g., 1,200 written as 12,000).
  \item \term{Omission}: an entry is left out of the ledger or the trial balance.
  \item \term{Commission}: a correct amount is entered on the wrong side (debit instead of credit or vice‑versa).
  \item \term{Double entry error}: the same amount is posted to the same side of two accounts.
\end{itemize}

Techniques to locate the error:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Re‑add the debit and credit columns to confirm the mismatch.
  \item Check that each ledger posting follows the double‑entry rule (debits = credits).
  \item Verify the arithmetic of each individual account balance.
  \item Look for numbers that differ by a factor of 10 (slide errors) or by a simple reversal (transposition).
  \item Review the journal entries to ensure no omission or commission.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}
A trial balance shows total debits $15,340$ and total credits $15,040$. The $300$ difference suggests a slide error. Examining the ledger, the Supplies account was recorded as $1,200$ instead of $12,000$. Correcting the entry changes the debit total to $16,140$, now matching the credit total of $16,140$ after also correcting a missed $1,100$ credit entry for Services Revenue.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Prepare a trial balance from the following ledger balances. If the totals do not match, identify the type of error and correct it.

\begin{tabular}{lrr}
Account & Debit & Credit\\
Bank & 8,500 & \\
Inventory & 3,200 & \\
Equipment & 6,000 & \\
Accounts Payable & & 5,700\\
Revenue & & 10,000\\
\end{tabular}
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Create a trial balance using the ledger balances: Cash $4,500$, Rent Expense $1,200$, Salaries Payable $2,300$, Capital $3,400$.
  \item A trial balance shows debits $9,850$ and credits $9,650$. Suggest two possible errors that could cause the $200$ difference.
  \item An accountant discovers that the Supplies account was entered as $2,500$ instead of $25,000$. Explain how this slide error affects the trial balance and how to fix it.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Trial balance & List of all ledger balances with separate debit and credit columns; totals should be equal. \\
Transposition error & Digits reversed, causing a small difference (often divisible by 9). \\
Slide error & A digit moved one place value, usually causing a difference of a factor of 10. \\
Omission & Failure to record a transaction or to include an account in the trial balance. \\
Commission & Correct amount placed on the wrong side of the trial balance. \\
Double‑entry error & Posting the same amount to the same side of two accounts, breaking the debit‑credit equality. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{6}
\section{The Income Statement}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item identify the main components of an income statement;
  \item calculate \term{revenues} and distinguish them from other items;
  \item compute \term{expenses} and understand their categories;
  \item determine \term{gross profit} and explain its meaning;
  \item determine \term{net profit} and explain its significance.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Revenues}

\term{Revenues} are the total amount of money earned from the core business activities during a period. For a retailer, revenues are the sales of goods; for a service firm, they are fees earned.

\begin{example}
A coffee shop sells 1,200 cups at \$3 each in one month. Its revenue is $1,200 \times 3 = \$3{,}600$.
\end{example}

\subsection{Expenses}

\term{Expenses} are the costs incurred to generate revenues. They include cost of goods sold, salaries, rent, utilities, and depreciation. Expenses are recorded in the period they are incurred.

\begin{example}
The same coffee shop pays \$1,200 for coffee beans, \$800 for wages, and \$200 for rent in the month. Total expenses are \$1,200 + \$800 + \$200 = \$2{,}200.
\end{example}

\subsection{Gross profit}

\term{Gross profit} is the difference between revenues and the cost of goods sold (COGS). It shows how much money remains after covering the direct cost of producing the sold items.

\[
\text{Gross profit} = \text{Revenues} - \text{Cost of goods sold}
\]

\begin{example}
If the coffee shop’s revenue is \$3,600 and the cost of coffee beans (COGS) is \$1,200, then gross profit = \$3,600 - \$1,200 = \$2{,}400.
\end{example}

\subsection{Net profit}

\term{Net profit} (or net income) is the amount left after all expenses, including operating costs, interest, and taxes, are deducted from revenues. It represents the final profitability of the business.

\[
\text{Net profit} = \text{Revenues} - \text{All expenses}
\]

\begin{example}
Using the coffee shop figures: revenue \$3,600, total expenses \$2,200. Net profit = \$3,600 - \$2,200 = \$1{,}400.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} A company reports \$50,000 revenue, \$30,000 cost of goods sold, and total expenses of \$45,000. What are its gross profit and net profit?
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item A bakery sells 5,000 loaves at \$2 each. Calculate its revenue.
  \item The bakery’s cost of flour and ingredients is \$3,000, wages \$2,500, rent \$1,200, and utilities \$300. Compute total expenses.
  \item Using the numbers above, find the bakery’s gross profit and net profit.
  \item Explain why gross profit is useful for assessing production efficiency, while net profit reflects overall business performance.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Revenues & Money earned from primary business activities during a period. \\
Expenses & All costs incurred to earn revenues, recorded in the period they arise. \\
Gross profit & Revenue minus cost of goods sold; shows profit from core production. \\
Net profit & Revenue minus all expenses; the final profit after all costs and taxes. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{7}
\section{The Balance Sheet}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define \term{assets}, \term{liabilities} and \term{equity};
  \item classify balance‑sheet items as current or non‑current;
  \item explain how the three sections relate to the accounting equation;
  \item read a simple balance sheet and identify each component.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Assets}

\term{Assets} are resources owned by a business that are expected to generate future economic benefits. They are recorded on the left side of the balance sheet.

Assets are divided into two categories:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Current assets}: cash or items that will be converted to cash, sold, or consumed within one year. Examples: cash, accounts receivable, inventory.
  \item \term{Non‑current assets}: resources that provide benefits for more than one year. Examples: property, plant and equipment, patents.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A retailer has \$10,000 in cash, \$5,000 of inventory, and a delivery van worth \$20,000. Cash and inventory are current assets; the van is a non‑current asset.
\end{example}

\subsection{Liabilities}

\term{Liabilities} are obligations of the business to transfer economic resources to others. They appear on the right side of the balance sheet.

Liabilities are also split into:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Current liabilities}: debts that must be settled within one year, such as accounts payable, short‑term loans, and taxes payable.
  \item \term{Non‑current liabilities}: obligations that extend beyond one year, such as long‑term bank loans and bonds payable.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A company owes \$8,000 to suppliers (current liability) and has a bank loan of \$50,000 payable over five years (non‑current liability).
\end{example}

\subsection{Equity}

\term{Equity} (or owners’ equity) represents the residual interest in the assets after deducting liabilities. It is the owners’ claim on the business.

Equity includes:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Share capital}: money invested by owners.
  \item \term{Retained earnings}: accumulated profits that have not been distributed as dividends.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
If a business has assets of \$100,000 and liabilities of \$60,000, equity equals \$40,000. This may consist of \$20,000 share capital and \$20,000 retained earnings.
\end{example}

\subsection{Current and Non‑Current Items}

The distinction between current and non‑current items helps users assess liquidity and long‑term solvency.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Current items} are expected to be realised or settled within the operating cycle (usually one year). They indicate short‑term financial health.
  \item \textbf{Non‑current items} relate to the long‑term structure of the business and affect its ability to generate future earnings.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
On a balance sheet, the current section lists cash, accounts receivable and inventory, while the non‑current section lists property, plant, equipment and long‑term debt.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} For each line item on a balance sheet, ask: Will this resource be turned into cash or settled within one year? If yes, classify it as current; if no, classify it as non‑current. Then verify that Assets = Liabilities + Equity.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item List three examples of current assets and three examples of non‑current assets.
  \item A company reports \$120,000 in assets, \$70,000 in liabilities, and \$50,000 in equity. Verify the accounting equation.
  \item Identify whether each of the following is current or non‑current: (a) a one‑year bank loan, (b) a five‑year mortgage, (c) inventory, (d) a patent.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Assets & Resources owned by the business expected to provide future economic benefits. \\
Current assets & Assets expected to be realised or used within one year. \\
Non‑current assets & Assets providing benefits for more than one year. \\
Liabilities & Obligations to transfer resources to others. \\
Current liabilities & Debts due within one year. \\
Non‑current liabilities & Debts due after one year. \\
Equity & Owners’ residual interest in the assets after liabilities are deducted. \\
Share capital & Money invested by owners in exchange for ownership. \\
Retained earnings & Cumulative profits retained in the business. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{8}
\section{Cash Flow Basics}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item identify the three categories of cash‑flow activities;
  \item classify typical transactions as operating, investing or financing;
  \item read a simple cash flow statement and explain the source of each cash flow.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Operating activities}

\term{Operating activities} are the cash inflows and outflows that arise from the core business operations. They include cash received from customers, cash paid to suppliers, wages, taxes and interest.

\begin{example}
A retailer sells goods for cash $5{,}000$ and pays $3{,}200$ to suppliers and $800$ in wages during the month. The net cash from operating activities is $5{,}000-3{,}200-800 = 1{,}000$.
\end{example}

\subsection{Investing activities}

\term{Investing activities} involve cash flows related to the acquisition or disposal of long‑term assets such as property, plant, equipment and investments. Buying a machine is a cash outflow; selling a piece of equipment is a cash inflow.

\begin{example}
A company purchases a delivery van for $20{,}000$ and sells an old computer for $2{,}000$. The net cash used in investing activities is $20{,}000-2{,}000 = 18{,}000$ (outflow).
\end{example}

\subsection{Financing activities}

\term{Financing activities} are cash flows that result from changes in the firm’s capital structure. They include issuing or repurchasing shares, borrowing or repaying loans, and paying dividends.

\begin{example}
The firm issues new shares and receives $15{,}000$ cash, then repays a bank loan of $5{,}000$. Net cash from financing activities is $15{,}000-5{,}000 = 10{,}000$ (inflow).
\end{example}

\subsection{Reading a cash flow statement}

A cash flow statement presents the three activity sections in order: operating, investing, financing. The total net cash change is the sum of the three sections. Positive net cash means the cash balance increased; negative means it decreased.

\begin{example}
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Net cash from operating activities} &= +1{,}000 \\
\text{Net cash used in investing activities} &= -18{,}000 \\
\text{Net cash from financing activities} &= +10{,}000 \\
\hline
\text{Net change in cash} &= -7{,}000
\end{aligned}
\]
The statement shows the firm’s cash balance fell by $7{,}000$ during the period.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} For each of the following transactions decide whether it belongs to operating, investing or financing activities, and state whether it is a cash inflow or outflow.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item List three cash inflows that would be classified as operating activities.
  \item A firm buys a building for $120{,}000$ and later sells a piece of equipment for $8{,}000$. Show the net cash flow from investing activities.
  \item Explain how a cash flow statement helps an investor assess a company’s liquidity.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Operating activities & Cash flows from the day‑to‑day business operations, such as sales and payments to suppliers. \\
Investing activities & Cash flows from buying or selling long‑term assets and investments. \\
Financing activities & Cash flows from changes in equity and debt, including dividends and loan repayments. \\
Cash flow statement & Financial report that summarises cash inflows and outflows by activity category for a period. \\
Net cash change & The sum of cash from operating, investing and financing activities; indicates increase or decrease in cash balance. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
